  </div>

  <!-- Video Section -->
  <div class="video-section">
    <div class="video-wrapper">
      <!-- 
        Usage:
        1. Local video file: Place video file in public/ directory, then specify path in src, e.g.:
           <video src={assetPath('videos/intro.mp4')} controls class="video-player"></video>
        
        2. External video link (YouTube, etc.): Use iframe, e.g.:
           <iframe src="https://www.youtube.com/embed/VIDEO_ID" frameborder="0" allowfullscreen class="video-player"></iframe>
        
        3. Currently displaying placeholder, uncomment and replace the code above to use video
      -->
      <div class="video-placeholder">
        <div class="video-placeholder-icon">
          <svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round">
            <polygon points="5 3 19 12 5 21 5 3"></polygon>
          </svg>
        </div>
        <p class="video-placeholder-text">Video Placeholder - Please add video file or video link</p>
      </div>
      <!-- 
        Uncomment the code below and modify the path/link to use video:
        <video src={assetPath('videos/intro.mp4')} controls class="video-player"></video>
        Or use iframe:
        <iframe src="YOUR_VIDEO_URL" frameborder="0" allowfullscreen class="video-player"></iframe>
      -->
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="hero-qa">
    <!-- Theory of Space Definition Section -->
    <div class="qa-item">
      <h3 class="qa-question">What is <span class="tos-text">Theory of Space</span>?</h3>
      <p class="qa-answer">
        <span class="tos-text">Theory of Space</span> is an agent's ability to actively construct, update, and utilize a globally consistent spatial belief under partial observability. It requires integrating partial observations through self-directed exploration, revising belief when new evidence conflicts with prior belief, and using the current belief to support spatial tasks and guide future exploration. We evaluate <span class="tos-text">Theory of Space</span> as an active loop of exploration, belief construction, and belief utilization.
      </p>
      <div class="qa-figure">
        <div class="qa-image-wrapper">
          <img
            src={assetPath('tos_def.png')}
            alt="Theory of Space definition"
            class="qa-image"
            loading="lazy"
          />
        </div>
        <p class="qa-caption"><span class="tos-text">Theory of Space</span> decomposes into constructing, updating, and utilizing an internal spatial belief.</p>
      </div>

      <p class="qa-answer">
        To operationalize this definition, we view <span class="tos-text">Theory of Space</span> as maintaining an internal spatial belief that is not directly observed. The agent actively explores to collect partial observations, and the model must integrate this exploration history into a coherent spatial belief that supports downstream spatial reasoning and guides future exploration.
      </p>
    </div>

    <!-- Why Theory of Space Section -->
    <div class="qa-item">
      <h3 class="qa-question">Why <span class="tos-text">Theory of Space</span>?</h3>
      <p class="qa-answer">
        Most evaluations of foundation models measure passive spatial reasoning from fixed observations or scripted trajectories. This setting does not test whether a model can build spatial belief when it must decide what to observe next under partial observability. Our results show a consistent passive to active gap across models in both text based and vision based environments. This gap motivates <span class="tos-text">Theory of Space</span> as a direct evaluation of active spatial belief construction beyond downstream answers.
      </p>
      <div class="qa-gap-figures">
        <div class="qa-gap-figure">
          <div class="qa-image-wrapper">
            <img
              src={assetPath('tos_gap_vision_web.png')}
              alt="Vision world gap"
              class="qa-image"
              loading="lazy"
            />
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="qa-gap-figure">
          <div class="qa-image-wrapper">
            <img
              src={assetPath('tos_gap_text_web.png')}
              alt="Text world gap"
              class="qa-image"
              loading="lazy"
            />
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <p class="qa-caption" style="text-align: center; margin-top: 1rem;">
        Models perform substantially better under passive logs than under active exploration.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="qa-item">
      <h3 class="qa-question">What is the challenge?</h3>
      <p class="qa-answer">
        The challenge is active decision making under partial observability: the agent must decide what to observe next while building a globally consistent spatial belief.
      </p>
      <div class="qa-figure">
        <div class="qa-image-wrapper">
          <img
            src={assetPath('explore.jpg')}
            alt="Active exploration under partial observability"
            class="qa-image"
            loading="lazy"
          />
        </div>
        <p class="qa-caption">Active exploration under partial observability requires choosing informative observations to reduce uncertainty.</p>
      </div>
      <p class="qa-answer">
        This requires the agent to:
      </p>
      <ul class="qa-list">
        <li>Identify uncertainty in its current belief and target unresolved ambiguities.</li>
        <li>Choose actions that efficiently reduce uncertainty, rather than merely increasing coverage.</li>
        <li>Integrate egocentric observations over time into a coherent global belief, while remaining robust to perceptual noise in vision.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
    <div class="qa-item">
      <h3 class="qa-question">What do we do?</h3>
      <p class="qa-answer">
        We implement <span class="tos-text">Theory of Space</span> in parallel text based and vision based environments under partial observability, enabling controlled cross modality comparisons.
      </p>
      
      <!-- Block 1: Task Suite -->
      <div class="qa-block">
        <div class="qa-block-title">
          <span class="qa-bullet">•</span>
          <span class="qa-block-label">Task Suite</span>
        </div>
        <div class="qa-block-content">
          <p class="qa-answer">
            We design a spatial task suite to evaluate belief utilization at two complementary levels. Route level tasks test egocentric, path based reasoning over local observations, while survey level tasks test allocentric, map like reasoning that supports global layout understanding and viewpoint transformation.
          </p>
          <p class="qa-answer" style="margin-top: 1.5rem;">
            To probe survey level belief, we evaluate whether a model can form and use an allocentric map, infer unseen views, and reason over geometric transformations beyond familiar paths.
          </p>
          <p class="qa-answer" style="margin-top: 1rem;">
            Route level tasks evaluate egocentric belief and path based spatial reasoning.
          </p>
          <p class="qa-answer" style="margin-top: 0.75rem;">
            Survey level tasks evaluate allocentric belief through global mapping and viewpoint transformation.
          </p>
          <p class="qa-answer" style="margin-top: 1.5rem; margin-bottom: 0;">
            We additionally evaluate belief updating via False Belief under covert environment changes.
          </p>
          <div class="qa-figure" style="margin-top: 1.5rem;">
            <div class="qa-image-wrapper">
              <img
                src={assetPath('tos_eval.png')}
                alt="Task suite for Theory of Space"
                class="qa-image"
                loading="lazy"
              />
            </div>
            <p class="qa-caption">Task suite for <span class="tos-text">Theory of Space</span> covering route-level and survey-level belief.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
      
      <!-- Block 2: Protocols -->
      <div class="qa-block" style="margin-top: -1rem;">
        <div class="qa-block-title">
          <span class="qa-bullet">•</span>
          <span class="qa-block-label">Protocols</span>
        </div>
        <div class="qa-block-content">
          <div class="qa-protocols-grid">
            <div class="qa-protocol-item">
              <h4 class="qa-protocol-title">Active Exploration</h4>
              <p class="qa-protocol-description">The agent autonomously selects actions to gather informative observations under partial observability.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="qa-protocol-item">
              <h4 class="qa-protocol-title">Passive Comprehension</h4>
              <p class="qa-protocol-description">The model reasons from standardized observation logs generated by scripted proxy explorers, decoupling exploration strategy from belief inference.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
      
      <!-- Block 3: Probing Internal Belief -->
      <div class="qa-block">
        <div class="qa-block-title">
          <span class="qa-bullet">•</span>
          <span class="qa-block-label">Probing Internal Belief</span>
        </div>
        <div class="qa-block-content">
          <p class="qa-answer">
            Beyond downstream accuracy, we explicitly probe internal spatial belief by requiring the model to externalize a cognitive map and uncertainty during exploration. This shifts evaluation from only final answers to the belief construction process itself, enabling direct assessment of belief quality and failure diagnosis, such as inconsistencies across local and global structure or unstable updates over time.
          </p>
          <div class="qa-figure" style="margin-top: 2.5rem;">
            <div class="qa-image-wrapper">
              <img
                src={assetPath('spatial_belief.png')}
                alt="Spatial belief probing"
                class="qa-image"
                loading="lazy"
              />
            </div>
            <p class="qa-caption">Spatial belief probing: externalize a cognitive map from exploration history and identify unexplored regions.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>

      
    </div>
  </div>

  

</section> 

<script>
  // Format numbers to show 'k' for thousands
  function formatNumber(num: number) {
    return num > 999 ? (num / 1000).toFixed(1) + 'k' : num;
  }

  // Fetch GitHub stats directly
  async function fetchGitHubStats() {
    try {
      // Try to use default numbers immediately
      const starsElement = document.getElementById('hero-stars-count');
      const forksElement = document.getElementById('hero-forks-count');
      
      // Fetch latest stats from GitHub
      const response = await fetch('https://api.github.com/repos/williamzhangNU/VAGEN');
      const data = await response.json();
      
      const stars = data.stargazers_count;
      const forks = data.forks_count;
      
      // Update stars count if available and different from default
      if (starsElement && stars !== undefined) {
        const formattedStars = formatNumber(stars);
        if (formattedStars !== '1.4k') {
          starsElement.textContent = formattedStars;
        }
      }
      
      // Update forks count if available and different from default
      if (forksElement && forks !== undefined) {
        const formattedForks = formatNumber(forks);
        if (formattedForks !== '105') {
          forksElement.textContent = formattedForks;
        }
      }
    } catch (error) {
      console.error('Error fetching GitHub stats:', error);
      // Keep default values on error
    }
  }

  // Run when page is loaded
  document.addEventListener('DOMContentLoaded', fetchGitHubStats);
</script> 

<style>
  .cta-button {
    display: inline-flex;
    align-items: center;
    gap: 12px;
    padding: 12px 24px;
    background: linear-gradient(45deg, #2a2f6c, #4b4f8f);
    border: none;
    border-radius: 50px;
    color: white;
    font-size: 18px;
    font-weight: 600;
    text-decoration: none;
    transition: all 0.3s ease;
    box-shadow: 0 4px 15px rgba(42, 47, 108, 0.2);
  }

  .cta-button:hover {
    transform: translateY(-2px);
    box-shadow: 0 6px 20px rgba(42, 47, 108, 0.3);
    text-decoration: none;
  }

  .button-icon {
    width: 24px;
    height: 24px;
    fill: currentColor;
  }

  .button-text {
    margin-right: 12px;
  }

  .repo-stats {
    display: flex;
    gap: 16px;
    padding-left: 16px;
    border-left: 1px solid rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.3);
  }

  .stat-item {
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    gap: 6px;
    color: white;
    font-size: 16px;
  }

